{"name":"White Radish","hex":"#e2e8cf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white radish","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of daikon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_radish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Daikon","blocks":[{"text":"Daikon or mooli is a mild-flavored winter radish usually characterized by fast-growing leaves and a long, white, napiform root. Native to continental East Asia, daikon and its cultivars are now harvested and consumed globally. In some locations, daikon is left unharvested to loosen compacted soils and recover nutrients."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Different_kinds_of_White_Radish.png/330px-Different_kinds_of_White_Radish.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daikon","wikidata":"Q7224757"},{"title":"Radish","blocks":[{"text":"The radish is a flowering plant in the mustard family, Brassicaceae. Its large taproot is commonly used as a root vegetable, although the entire plant is edible and its leaves are sometimes used as a leaf vegetable. Domesticated in Asia, radishes are grown and consumed globally. The radish is sometimes considered to form a species complex with the wild radish and instead treated as the subspecies Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Radish_3371103037_4ab07db0bf_o.jpg/330px-Radish_3371103037_4ab07db0bf_o.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radish","wikidata":"Q7224565"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Daikon%2C_Nara_Prefecture%2C_Japan.jpg/500px-Daikon%2C_Nara_Prefecture%2C_Japan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Daikon%2C_Nara_Prefecture%2C_Japan.jpg/500px-Daikon%2C_Nara_Prefecture%2C_Japan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaikon%2C_Nara_Prefecture%2C_Japan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._21%2C_page_022_-_%E7%AB%A0%E9%AD%9A%E8%91%8D%2C_%E7%A7%A6%E9%87%8E%E8%91%8D%2C_%E9%BC%A0%E8%91%8D_-_Raphanus_raphanistrum_subsp._sativus_%28L.%29_Domin_-_%E8%91%9B%E7%95%91%E8%91%8D%2C_%E8%BE%9B%E8%91%8D_-_idem%2C_1804.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._21%2C_page_022_-_%E7%AB%A0%E9%AD%9A%E8%91%8D%2C_%E7%A7%A6%E9%87%8E%E8%91%8D%2C_%E9%BC%A0%E8%91%8D_-_Raphanus_raphanistrum_subsp._sativus_%28L.%29_Domin_-_%E8%91%9B%E7%95%91%E8%91%8D%2C_%E8%BE%9B%E8%91%8D_-_idem%2C_1804.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Varieties of Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus from the Seikei Zusetsu agricultural encyclopedia, 1804","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._21%2C_page_022_-_%E7%AB%A0%E9%AD%9A%E8%91%